WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.500
hi it's Michelle from lab muffin beauty
science chemistry PhD and skincare nerd

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.580
today we're going to be talking about
skincare when it comes to wearing masks

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.360
I had a lot of requests about this topic
as well as how to look after your hands

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.539
when you're washing your hands a lot
which I talked about in my last video

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:21.960
and again I went to lots of
dermatologists and asked them for their

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.609
advice on dealing with skin issues when
you're wearing a mask skin problems can

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:30.119
come up when you wear any sort of
covering on your face this can happen

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:33.149
with cloth masks which you might wear
when you're going to the store this is

00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:36.989
one that my aunt sewed for me or it
can happen with surgical masks which a

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:40.649
lot of health care workers wear on a
more extreme level it can happen with

00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:44.700
hard respirators like this one which I
wore during the bush fires last year in

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.750
Australia a lot of health care workers
who work closely with covid patients

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.469
will be wearing these for hours on end
so here's a guide to the common skin

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:56.010
issues that might happen when you're
wearing a mask and how to deal with them

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:59.489
if you like the sort of video give it
a thumbs up subscribe to my channel and

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:07.170
click the notification bell so you don't
miss any videos first off let's talk

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.860
about the sorts of skin issues that you
might see when you're wearing a mask for

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.060
a long period of time with a looser mask
the main issue is going to be skin

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.799
irritation this includes dryness itching
and redness this is called irritant

00:01:19.799 --> 00:01:23.430
contact dermatitis and this happens
because of the extra heat from the mask

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:28.110
the fact that your breath and your sweat
are making it very moist and humid under

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.340
the mask plus there's the friction of
the mask rubbing against your skin you

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.090
can also get acne from the material
rubbing against your skin and physically

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:41.070
blocking your pores this is called acne
mechanica respirators are a lot harder

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:45.090
and they fit much more tightly against
your skin this means you can end up with

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:49.710
hives and pressure sores raw skin and
blisters different components of the

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:54.869
mask like adhesives rubber straps and
even free formaldehyde from n95

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:59.790
respirators these can all cause irritant
and allergic reactions this means red

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:04.649
and inflamed skin itching and rashes so
here's some advice for what to do when

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:07.610
you're wearing the mask

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:14.120
firstly it's a good idea to avoid heavy
makeup and foundation under the mask

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.780
the extra pressure and friction from
the mask can mean that you get spots and

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.980
acne even if you don't normally react to
these products you can also use a

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.600
moisturizer or a barrier cream like zinc
oxide or dimethicone based 3M Cavilon

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.620
under the mask especially on the spots
where the mask rubs and causes pressure

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:34.880
this is generally on your nose the sides
of your face and under your chin be

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.569
careful if you're a health care worker
using a respirator if you're using these

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:41.930
products right before you put on the
mask it can affect the quality of the

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.130
seal so check if this applies to you you
can also use protective dressings like

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:50.720
hydrocolloid bandages and skin tapes on
areas where the mask rubs or if you have

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.860
a wound Dr Anjali Mahto recommends
removing pressure every 1 to 2 hours

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.880
where possible here are some tricks from
Dr Papri for relieving pressure from

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.200
behind your ears you can use a paper
clip or a strap to turn the loops that

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.569
go behind your ears into a strap that
goes around your whole head

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:11.439
if you have longer hair you can also
wear Princess Leia buns and put the

00:03:11.439 --> 00:03:15.939
loops around those make sure you monitor
your skin for signs of infection and

00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:18.689
seek medical help

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:24.849
here are some products that might be
useful in a skincare routine if your

00:03:24.849 --> 00:03:29.200
skin's been irritated by masks it's a
good idea to use a mild soap free facial

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.670
cleanser or makeup remover wipes right
after you've taken off the mask Dr

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:37.870
Anjali Mahto recommends using lukewarm
water to wash your face not water that's

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.569
too hot or too cold because that can
cause irritation

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:44.230
you should also pat your face dry with a
towel rather than rubbing if you do

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.310
develop acne some good anti acne
ingredients are benzoyl peroxide

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:54.760
salicylic acid azelaic acid sulfur and
retinoids if your skin is easily

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:59.859
irritated by benzoyl peroxide Dr Papri
recommends washing it off after 20 to 30

00:03:59.859 --> 00:04:03.939
minutes which is called short contact
this can help reduce irritation and

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:08.829
dryness exfoliants and retinoids work
really well if you have acne but they do

00:04:08.829 --> 00:04:12.609
cause a lot of irritation so if your
skin's already irritated it's probably

00:04:12.609 --> 00:04:17.229
best to avoid these or use gentler
versions in terms of moisturizers Dr

00:04:17.229 --> 00:04:21.729
Adeline recommends moisturizers which
contains glycerin ceramides hyaluronic

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:26.080
acid and niacinamide these are all very
gentle on your skin and good for helping

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.159
barrier repair you can also use thick
emollients like Vaseline and aquaphor

00:04:30.159 --> 00:04:34.300
to help your skin heal you can also try
using dimethicone based barrier creams

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:39.430
or products like la roche-posay cicaplast
baume b5 if you have a rash a few

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:43.690
dermatologists also recommended trying
some over-the-counter 1% hydrocortisone

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:47.620
cream you can use a thin layer twice a
day for a week or two and this will

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:51.460
reduce itching redness and inflammation
you can also talk to a doctor who can

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:55.150
prescribe a stronger steroid cream
another problem that can develop after a

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.780
while is post inflammatory
hyperpigmentation these are patches of

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.380
brown pigment that develop after your
skin's healed if you want more tips on

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:06.009
how to fade this you can check out my
video on hyperpigmentation if your skin

00:05:06.009 --> 00:05:09.580
is very irritated then it's a good idea
to get rid of irritating products from

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.900
your routine until you stop wearing
masks so much so things like exfoliants

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.620
and retinoids

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:22.690
the people I spoke to also gave me some
extra tips for wearing masks remember

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:26.020
that the front of your mask gets
contaminated after you've worn it so

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.720
don't touch it
Dr Papri recommends that if you're

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.800
planning to reuse a mask you can store
it in a Tupperware container put a

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.210
Tupperware container over your mask take
off the straps and then you can store

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:40.630
the mask in the Tupperware container
when you reapply it put the Tupperware

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:44.950
container on your face and put the
straps back on it's also a good idea to

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:48.700
wash your hands before you put on a mask
and after you take it off I hope you

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:52.090
found this video useful if you did you
can give it a thumbs up and subscribe to

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:55.990
my channel leave me a comment if you
have any more tips on using masks thank

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:59.230
you to all of the experts who shared
their tips with me I've put their social

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:02.050
media accounts here so give them a
follow if you want to find out more

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:05.020
about the science behind beauty products
you can follow me on Instagram and check

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:10.290
out my blog see you next time for more
